from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "6. “The Signs of an Apostle”"),
("p", "Claim: Paul names signs, wonders and mighty deeds as “the signs of an apostle,” and the book of Acts records them in the hands of the Twelve and in the hands of Paul. The apostolic office, as a witness authenticated by signs, ends with the sign-program at Acts 28. Paul’s authority does not end there, because it rests on the revelation committed to him and not on the signs, and it continues in the letters written after the record of Acts closes. This chapter argues that position from the passages."),
("sub", "6.1 What Paul says"),
("p", "Writing to Corinth, where certain men set themselves above him, Paul says: “For I suppose I was not a whit behind the very chiefest apostles” (2 Corinthians 11:5). In the twelfth chapter he gives the proof: “Truly the signs of an apostle were wrought among you in all patience, in signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds” (2 Corinthians 12:12). Three words are joined: signs, wonders and mighty deeds. They are the same three words that stand in Romans 15:19 (“mighty signs and wonders”) and in Hebrews 2:4 (“signs and wonders, and with divers miracles”)."),
("p", "The verse does not say that signs make an apostle. It says that the signs of an apostle were wrought among the Corinthians. The signs are the mark by which the claim is known, as the seal is the mark by which a letter is known. Paul makes the claim to men who doubted it, and the signs are what he offers. The verse puts the signs under the office, as its mark, and it does not put the office under the signs."),
("tbl", "The signs of an apostle.", [
    ("2 Corinthians 11:5", T("2 Cor 11:5"), "Paul is not a whit behind the very chiefest apostles."),
    ("2 Corinthians 12:11, 12", T("2 Cor 12:11-12"), "The signs of an apostle were wrought among the Corinthians: signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds."),
    ("Romans 15:18, 19", T("Rom 15:18-19"), "Christ wrought by Paul mighty signs and wonders by the power of the Spirit of God."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "6.2 What Acts records"),
("p", "The book of Acts records signs in the hands of both. In Jerusalem, “by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people” (Acts 5:12). At Iconium the Lord “gave testimony unto the word of his grace, and granted signs and wonders to be done by their hands,” the hands of Barnabas and Paul (Acts 14:3). At Ephesus, “God wrought special miracles by the hands of Paul: so that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them” (Acts 19:11, 12)."),
("p", "These signs and the others in Acts 9 to 28 are overlap phenomena. They are recorded in settings shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, which remained operative throughout Acts 9 to 28 even as the Mystery Program began. Acts 9 to 28 is the overlap; where Paul or his companions are the ones working signs, the volume reports the text and does not assign the event to a single program. Paul’s participation does not convert the signs into Prophecy Program content, and it does not make them Mystery content."),
("sub", "6.3 The last sign recorded"),
("p", "The last signs the record gives are on Malta, in the last chapter. “It came to pass, that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever and of a bloody flux: to whom Paul entered in, and prayed, and laid his hands on him, and healed him. So when this was done, others also, which had diseases in the island, came, and were healed” (Acts 28:8, 9). No sign is recorded after these verses. The volume states that the record of signs ends in Acts 28. It does not argue from the silence that signs ended, and it does not say what happened elsewhere. It says that the book of Acts, which recorded them in Jerusalem, in Galatia, in Ephesus and on Malta, records none after this chapter."),
("p", "The suspension of Israel’s program at Acts 28 is argued in Acts Commentary Volume 5, and this volume takes it from there. With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically. Signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. The sentence does not deny that God can heal, and it makes no statement about his power. It governs the programs."),
("sub", "6.4 The office and the signs end together"),
("p", "The signs of an apostle were the mark of the apostolic office as a witness. The Twelve were sent to Israel with power “to heal sicknesses, and to cast out devils” (Mark 3:15), and Paul’s signs are “the signs of an apostle” among the Gentiles and in the synagogues. If the sign-program ceases programmatically at Acts 28, the office that it authenticated ceases with it. The sign and the office have one ground, and when the ground is removed, both go. The volume states this as its position. It is an inference from the passages, and it is the thesis of the volume: the apostolic office as a sign-authenticated witness ends with the sign-program at Acts 28."),
("p", "The inference has a limit, and the limit is the next section. The office that ends is the office as a witness authenticated by signs. What the apostle committed to writing does not end."),
("sub", "6.5 What continues: Paul’s authority in his letters"),
("p", "Paul’s authority does not rest on the signs. It rests on the source he states in Galatians 1:1 and 1:12: the Lord’s own commission and the revelation of Jesus Christ. He tells the Corinthians, “If any man think himself to be a prophet, or spiritual, let him acknowledge that the things that I write unto you are the commandments of the Lord” (1 Corinthians 14:37). The claim is that what he writes is the Lord’s commandment. The claim does not depend on a sign in the room."),
("p", "The letters in which Paul speaks of the mystery were written, in the volume’s reading, after the record of Acts closes: Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians and Philemon from prison, and the letters to Timothy and Titus. In them Paul teaches the Body and gives it direction, and the church is told to hold what he delivered. In the second letter to Timothy he writes of a charge to be committed on: “And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also” (2 Timothy 2:2). What is committed on is what was heard and taught. The verse names no apostle in succession."),
("p", "That Paul’s authority continues in his post-Acts-28 letters is the volume’s position, and the dating of those letters to the years after the record of Acts is the volume’s reading of the order in which they were written. The reader who holds a different order will find that the argument of this section does not change: Paul’s authority is in his letters, whenever they were written, and the signs are not what it rests on."),
("sub", "6.6 Three passages that speak of signs"),
("p", "Three passages are often brought into the question, and the volume reads each in its own setting. The first is Mark 16:20, which ends the long ending of that Gospel: “And they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following. Amen.” The verse is read as written, with the textual question of Mark 16:9 to 20 left to Study 25. The verse says that the Lord confirmed the word of those who went forth with signs following, and it speaks of the men who had been with him."),
("p", "The second is Hebrews 2:3, 4, where the writer says that the great salvation “at the first began to be spoken by the Lord, and was confirmed unto us by them that heard him; God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will.” The letter is written to Hebrew readers, and the passage is read as written to them. The volume does not carry it to the Body. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting."),
("p", "The third is John 20:30, 31, where John gives his own purpose for the signs he recorded: “that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God.” The volume reports it as John’s stated purpose for his signs and does not read it as a statement on all signs."),
("tbl", "Three passages on signs, read in their own settings.", [
    ("Mark 16:20", T("Mark 16:20"), "The Lord working with those who went forth, confirming the word with signs following."),
    ("Hebrews 2:3, 4", T("Heb 2:3-4"), "Salvation confirmed by them that heard the Lord, God bearing witness with signs and wonders."),
    ("Acts 28:8, 9", T("Acts 28:8-9"), "The last signs the record gives: Paul on Malta."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Paul calls signs, wonders and mighty deeds “the signs of an apostle,” and puts them under the office as its mark.",
    "Acts records signs in the hands of the Twelve and of Paul, and the last signs it records are on Malta in Acts 28:8, 9.",
    "The apostolic office as a sign-authenticated witness ends with the sign-program at Acts 28.",
    "Paul’s authority rests on the Lord’s commission and the revelation committed to him, and it continues in his letters.",
    "Mark 16:20, Hebrews 2:3, 4 and John 20:30, 31 are read in their own settings and are not carried to the Body.",
  ]),
]
